IMPORT prelude

-- Registration of Criminals Act 1949 (Singapore) -- when a criminal record becomes spent (ss 7A, 7B, 7C, 7D, 7DA, Third Schedule),
-- what a spent record lets the individual say (s 7E), whose consent is needed to take identifying information or body samples
-- (s 8 "appropriate consent"), the refusal offences (ss 16, 27) and removal of records at death or age 100 (s 33).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/RCA1949.txt, with amendments
-- to S 608/2026 (in force 15 September 2026) shown.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 19 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to the provisions an ordinary person
-- with a conviction, or a person asked for fingerprints or a body sample, actually meets. Not encoded: the Registrar and the
-- register itself (ss 3-5), the powers to take identifying information and body samples (ss 9-15, 17-26) beyond the consent rule,
-- the court's inferences from refusal (s 28), the databases (ss 29-32), removal on acquittal or non-involvement and the appeal to a
-- Reviewing Tribunal (ss 34-46), foreign agencies and evidence (ss 47-51), the First Schedule list of registrable crimes, and
-- the Youth Court route of s 7DA(2) beyond a flag.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Registration of Criminals Act 1949 -- spent records`

-- A handful of offences, to show the Third Schedule test. The first five are listed in the Third Schedule (Part 1, Penal Code ss
-- 147, 304, 354, 363; Part 2, Misuse of Drugs Act 1973 s 5). Theft and cheating do not appear in the deposited Third Schedule.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `rioting`
    `culpable homicide not amounting to murder`
    `outraging of modesty`
    `kidnapping`
    `trafficking in controlled drugs`
    `theft`
    `cheating`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence is in the Third Schedule` IF
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `theft` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `cheating` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- The facts about one conviction within Singapore for a registrable crime, and the individual's position since.
DECLARE Conviction HAS
    `offence` IS AN Offence
    `months of imprisonment` IS A NUMBER
    `months of imprisonment in default of a fine` IS A NUMBER
    `fine in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `committed within 5 years of release from an approved institution or centre` IS A BOOLEAN
    `detained under the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act before the conviction` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has an unexecuted warrant of arrest` IS A BOOLEAN
    `number of convictions in the register` IS A NUMBER
    `had an earlier record spent or treated as spent` IS A BOOLEAN
    `years since the relevant date` IS A NUMBER
    `crime-free since the relevant date` IS A BOOLEAN
    `community sentence passed and completed` IS A BOOLEAN
    `Youth Court order ended or satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN
    `Commissioner granted treatment as spent` IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `7B(4) -- the relevant date`

-- s 7B(4) "relevant date": (a) where the sentence "does not include a term of imprisonment and is no longer subject to appeal, the
-- date on which the sentence was passed"; (b) where it "includes a term of imprisonment", "the date of his or her release from legal
-- custody"; (c) where imprisonment "has been wholly remitted or commuted to or substituted with a sentence other than a term of
-- imprisonment, the date of that remission, commutation or substituted sentence".
-- s 7A(1): "term of imprisonment" does not include imprisonment "in default of payment of a fine or penalty". s 7A(2): home
-- detention is legal custody. Dates are given as numbers (years); the "no longer subject to appeal" condition is not modelled.
GIVEN `the sentence includes a term of imprisonment` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the imprisonment was wholly remitted or commuted` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `date sentence passed` IS A NUMBER,
      `date released from custody` IS A NUMBER,
      `date of remission or commutation` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the relevant date` IS
    IF `the sentence includes a term of imprisonment` AND `the imprisonment was wholly remitted or commuted`
    THEN `date of remission or commutation`
    ELSE IF `the sentence includes a term of imprisonment`
         THEN `date released from custody`
         ELSE `date sentence passed`

§§ `7C and Third Schedule -- disqualification`

-- s 7C: disqualified if (a) the offence is in the Third Schedule; (b) the sentence included "(i) a term of imprisonment exceeding
-- 3 months; or (ii) a fine exceeding $2,000"; (c) the crime was committed "within 5 years after" release from an approved
-- institution (Misuse of Drugs Act) or approved centre (Intoxicating Substances Act); (d) detained or under police supervision under
-- s 30(1) Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act "at any time before that conviction"; (e) "for the time being" an unexecuted
-- warrant of arrest; (f) "records in the register of more than one conviction, whether or not those convictions arise from the same
-- particular occasion"; or (g) "any previous record" already spent under s 7B or treated as spent under s 7D.
-- Imprisonment in default of a fine is not a "term of imprisonment" (s 7A(1)), so it does not count under (b)(i).
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A Conviction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the individual is disqualified` IF
        `the offence is in the Third Schedule` (`the conviction`'s `offence`)
    OR  `the conviction`'s `months of imprisonment` GREATER THAN 3
    OR  `the conviction`'s `fine in dollars` GREATER THAN 2000
    OR  `the conviction`'s `committed within 5 years of release from an approved institution or centre`
    OR  `the conviction`'s `detained under the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act before the conviction`
    OR  `the conviction`'s `has an unexecuted warrant of arrest`
    OR  `the conviction`'s `number of convictions in the register` GREATER THAN 1
    OR  `the conviction`'s `had an earlier record spent or treated as spent`

§§ `7B -- spent by lapse of time`

-- s 7B(2): the record "becomes spent on the expiration of the crime-free period applicable to the individual, unless he or she is
-- disqualified under section 7C". (4) "crime-free period": "not less than 5 consecutive years starting from the relevant date",
-- during which the individual was not convicted of a registrable crime, not unlawfully at large, not detained or supervised under
-- the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act, and not under a drug supervision order or in an approved institution or centre.
-- (3): a spent record "is not revived by the subsequent conviction of the individual for any offence after the crime-free period".
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A Conviction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the record is spent by lapse of time` IF
        (NOT `the individual is disqualified` `the conviction`)
    AND `the conviction`'s `years since the relevant date` AT LEAST 5
    AND `the conviction`'s `crime-free since the relevant date`

§§ `7DA -- community sentences and Youth Court orders`

-- s 7DA(1): "Despite any provision in this Part", a record of a conviction for which a community sentence is passed "becomes spent on
-- the date on which the community sentence is completed". (2): a Youth Court order under s 49(1)(e)-(j) Children and Young Persons
-- Act 1993 -- the record becomes spent at the end of the supervision or detention, the day after a community sentence is completed,
-- or the day after the fine, damages or costs are paid.
-- "Despite any provision in this Part" is read here as overriding s 7C disqualification.
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A Conviction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the record is spent on completing the sentence` IF
        `the conviction`'s `community sentence passed and completed`
    OR  `the conviction`'s `Youth Court order ended or satisfied`

§§ `7D -- treated as spent on application`

-- s 7D(1): a disqualified individual "may, at any time, apply to the Commissioner of Police to have the record treated as spent".
-- (3) the Commissioner may refuse or grant; (4) having regard to the severity of the conviction, the severity of the sentence and
-- "the applicant's conduct subsequent to the conviction". The Commissioner's grant is taken as a fact.
GIVEN `the conviction` IS A Conviction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the individual may apply to the Commissioner` IF
    `the individual is disqualified` `the conviction`

GIVEN `the conviction` IS A Conviction
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the record is spent or treated as spent` IF
        `the record is spent by lapse of time` `the conviction`
    OR  `the record is spent on completing the sentence` `the conviction`
    OR  (`the individual may apply to the Commissioner` `the conviction` AND `the conviction`'s `Commissioner granted treatment as spent`)

§§ `7E -- what a spent record allows`

-- s 7E(1)(a): the individual "is deemed to have no record of that conviction, and it is lawful for him or her to answer a question
-- ... about his or her criminal record ... as if he or she had no record of that conviction". (1)(c): references in agreements
-- and documents are read the same way.
-- (2): (1)(a) and (c) do NOT apply to (a) an investigation into an offence; (b) a prosecution of the individual; (c) proceedings
-- before a court, including giving evidence and sentencing; (d) an application for an office, profession or vocation "from which
-- the individual may be disqualified under any written law by reason of his or her conviction".
-- (3): "Despite subsection (2)", (1)(a) and (c) apply to all of those where the individual is a child or young person.
DECLARE Setting IS ONE OF
    `a private employer's job application`
    `an insurance proposal form`
    `a police investigation`
    `a prosecution of the individual`
    `giving evidence in court`
    `an application to a profession the law bars convicted persons from`

GIVEN `the setting` IS A Setting
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the setting is excepted by s 7E(2)` IF
    CONSIDER `the setting`
    WHEN `a private employer's job application` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `an insurance proposal form` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the conviction` IS A Conviction, `the setting` IS A Setting, `the individual is a child or young person` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the individual may answer as if there were no record` IF
        `the record is spent or treated as spent` `the conviction`
    AND (    (NOT `the setting is excepted by s 7E(2)` `the setting`)
         OR  `the individual is a child or young person`)

§ `Registration of Criminals Act 1949 -- identification`

§§ `8 -- appropriate consent`

-- s 8 "appropriate consent": (a) aged 16 or over -- "the written consent of the individual"; (b) under 16 but at least 14 -- "the
-- written consent of both the individual and the individual's parent or guardian"; (c) under 14 -- "the written consent of the
-- individual's parent or guardian".
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the individual's own consent is needed` IF
    `age` AT LEAST 14

GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a parent's or guardian's consent is needed` IF
    `age` LESS THAN 16

§§ `16 and 27 -- refusal offences`

-- s 16(2): an individual within s 9(1), 10(1)/(2), 11(1)/(3)/(5), 12(1) or 13(1) commits an offence if, "without reasonable excuse",
-- he refuses to submit to finger impressions, photographs or a forensic procedure, refuses to provide identifying information, or
-- otherwise hinders or obstructs. (3): the offence is committed "regardless of whether" the information was obtained by reasonable
-- force under s 17. (4): fine up to $1,000, imprisonment up to one month, or both.
GIVEN `within the classes of s 16(1)` IS A BOOLEAN, `refused or obstructed` IS A BOOLEAN, `had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `taken anyway by reasonable force` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `refusing identifying information is an offence` IF
    `within the classes of s 16(1)` AND `refused or obstructed` AND (NOT `had a reasonable excuse`)

-- s 27(3): where the individual is 14 or 15, "both the parent or guardian of the individual and the individual" who refuse
-- appropriate consent for an invasive sample "each commits an offence". (4): under 14, the parent or guardian who refuses commits an
-- offence. Neither subsection reaches a parent of an individual aged 16 or over.
-- For an individual of 16 or over, s 27(2) makes refusing "to give a body sample" or "to allow a body sample to be taken" an
-- offence. Treating refusal of consent to an invasive sample as such a refusal is an INFERENCE; that case is not asserted.
GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the individual commits an offence by refusing invasive-sample consent` IF
    `age` AT LEAST 14

GIVEN `age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the parent commits an offence by refusing invasive-sample consent` IF
    `age` LESS THAN 16

-- s 16(4) and s 27(6): "a fine not exceeding $1,000 or ... imprisonment for a term not exceeding one month or ... both".
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `maximum fine for a refusal offence` IS 1000

GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `maximum months of imprisonment for a refusal offence` IS 1

§ `Registration of Criminals Act 1949 -- removal`

§§ `33 -- death or age 100`

-- s 33: the Registrar "must remove from the register, identification database and DNA database, all identifying information, records
-- and DNA information" relating to an individual (a) "whose death has been registered" under the Registration of Births and Deaths
-- Act 2021; or (b) who, the Registrar is satisfied, "has attained 100 years of age".
GIVEN `death registered` IS A BOOLEAN, `age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Registrar must remove all records` IF
    `death registered` OR `age` AT LEAST 100
